                        </enumeratedValue>
                     </enumeratedValues>
                  </field>
               </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
               <name>PRECNTR</name>
               <description>Prescaler Counter</description>
               <addressOffset>548</addressOffset>
               <access>read-write</access>
               <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
               <fields>
                  <field>
                     <name>CAPT</name>
                     <description>Prescaler counter capture strobe.

Write a 1 to CAPT to capture the value of the 16-bit prescaler counter into CNT. Read CNT to get the captured value. </description>
                     <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
                     <access>write-only</access>
                  </field>
                  <field>
                     <name>CNT</name>
                     <description>Prescaler counter value.

Write a 1 to CAPT to capture the value of the 16-bit prescaler counter into CNT. Read CNT to get the captured value. 

The read value gets 1 LSB uncertainty if the event source level rises when you release the reset.
The read value gets 1 LSB uncertainty if the event source level rises when you capture the prescaler counter.

Please note the following:
- The prescaler counter is reset to 3 by [TDC.PRECTL.RESET_N].
- The captured value is 3 when the number of rising edges on prescaler input is less than 3. Otherwise, captured value equals number of event pulses.

</description>
                     <bitRange>[15:0]</bitRange>
                     <access>read-only</access>
                  </field>
               </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
               <name>CNT</name>
               <description>WDT counter value register</description>
               <addressOffset>768</addressOffset>
               <access>read-write</access>
               <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
               <fields>
                  <field>
                     <name>VAL</name>
                     <description>Counter value.

A write to this field immediately starts (or restarts) the counter. It will count down from the written value.
If the counter reaches 0, a reset will be generated.
A write value of 0 immediately generates a reset.

This field is only writable if not locked. See LOCK register.
Writing this field will automatically activate the lock.

A read returns the current value of the counter.</description>
                     <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                  </field>
               </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
               <name>TEST</name>
               <description>WDT test mode register</description>
               <addressOffset>772</addressOffset>
               <access>read-write</access>
               <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
               <fields>
                  <field>
                     <name>STALLEN</name>
                     <description>WDT stall enable

This field is only writable if not locked. See LOCK register.</description>
                     <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                     <enumeratedValues>
                        <enumeratedValue>
                           <name>EN</name>
                           <value>0x1</value>
                           <description>ENABLE

WDT stops counting while the CPU is stopped by a debugger.</description>
                        </enumeratedValue>
                        <enumeratedValue>
                           <name>DIS</name>
                           <value>0x0</value>
                           <description>DISABLE

WDT continues counting while the CPU is stopped by a debugger.</description>
                        </enumeratedValue>
                     </enumeratedValues>
                  </field>
               </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
               <name>LOCK</name>
               <description>WDT lock register</description>
               <addressOffset>776</addressOffset>
               <access>read-write</access>
               <resetValue>0x00000001</resetValue>
               <fields>
                  <field>
                     <name>STAT</name>
                     <description>A write with value 0x1ACCE551 unlocks the watchdog registers for write access.
A write with any other value locks the watchdog registers for write access.
Writing the CNT register will also lock the watchdog registers.

A read of this field returns the state of the lock (0=unlocked, 1=locked).</description>
                     <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                  </field>
               </fields>
            </register>
         </registers>
      </peripheral>
      <peripheral>
         <name>CLKCTL</name>
         <description>This component is the clock controller. Here SW can turn on and off IP clocks and read IP and system clock status.
 
</description>
         <baseAddress>0x40020000</baseAddress>
         <addressBlock>
            <offset>0x0</offset>
            <size>0x00001000</size>
            <usage>registers</usage>
         </addressBlock>
         <registers>
            <register>
               <name>DESC</name>
               <description>Description Register. 
This register provides IP module ID, revision information, instance index and standard MMR registers offset.</description>
               <addressOffset>0</addressOffset>
               <access>read-only</access>
               <resetValue>0x254f0000</resetValue>
               <fields>
                  <field>
                     <name>MODID</name>
                     <description>Module identifier used to uniquely identify this IP.</description>
                     <bitRange>[31:16]</bitRange>
                     <access>read-only</access>
                  </field>
                  <field>
                     <name>STDIPOFF</name>
                     <description>Standard IP MMR block offset. Standard IP MMRs are the set of from aggregated IRQ registers till DTB.

0: Standard IP MMRs do not exist
0x1-0xF: Standard IP MMRs begin at offset of (64*STDIPOFF from the base IP address)</description>
                     <bitRange>[15:12]</bitRange>
                     <access>read-only</access>
                  </field>
                  <field>
                     <name>INSTIDX</name>
                     <description>IP Instance ID number. If multiple instances of IP exist in the device, this field can identify the instance number (0-15).</description>
                     <bitRange>[11:8]</bitRange>
                     <access>read-only</access>
                  </field>
                  <field>
                     <name>MAJREV</name>
                     <description>Major revision of IP 0-15</description>
                     <bitRange>[7:4]</bitRange>
                     <access>read-only</access>
                  </field>
                  <field>
                     <name>MINREV</name>
                     <description>Minor revision of IP 0-15.</description>
                     <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
                     <access>read-only</access>
                  </field>
               </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
